"critique":"The primary focal point is ambiguous. The large 'The Skill Codex' title in the upper-left of the main content area competes directly with the skill tree matrix for attention, creating a split hierarchy where the user's eye doesn't know whether to read the header or explore the nodes. The sidebar is visually dense and vertically long, with the 'Attributes' section at the bottom feeling disconnected from the primary 'Level/XP' data, creating a flat vertical list rather than a prioritized stack. The 'Available Points' indicator (the 3 diamonds) is visually small and low-contrast relative to the 'Level 27' number, despite being the critical resource for the user's immediate action. The skill tree nodes themselves lack a clear visual distinction between 'locked,' 'available,' and 'purchased' states beyond the small number badges, making the reading order of the tree (which nodes are next) difficult to parse at a glance.",
"suggestion":"1. Reduce the scale of the 'The Skill Codex' title or move it to the top bar to establish the skill tree as the undisputed primary focal point. 2. Increase the visual weight of the 'Available Points' (the 3 diamonds) by making them larger or adding a subtle glow, as this is the key to the interaction. 3. Group the sidebar elements more tightly: keep Level/XP/Points as a primary 'Status' block and visually separate Attributes with more whitespace or a divider to create a clear secondary tier. 4. Enhance the state differentiation of the skill nodes: use a distinct border color or glow for 'available' nodes (those with the pulsing rings mentioned in the tooltip) to make the next possible actions immediately obvious without reading the small badges."
}

